import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — SeedMask"
  description="SeedMask builds Bitcoin and Kaspa-only hardware wallets with air-gapped signing and a 100% open-source stack. Backup code, wipe, Stateless, why SeedMask was born, and the path to launch."
>
  <section class="page-hero page-hero-learn">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Building a serious hardware wallet company</h1>
      <p>
        SeedMask is Bitcoin and Kaspa only — cold keys on device, watch-only Coordinator on desktop, and an honest path from firmware to funded team.
      </p>
      <div class="chain-row" style="margin-top: 22px">
        <span class="chip chip-logo">
          <img src="/branding/bitcoin-btc-logo.png" alt="" />
          Bitcoin
        </span>
        <span class="chip chip-logo">
          <img src="/branding/kaspa-kas-logo.png" alt="" />
          Kaspa
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <article class="origin-story" id="why-seedmask">
        <p class="eyebrow">Origin</p>
        <h2>Why was SeedMask born?</h2>
        <p class="origin-lead">
          Having crypto is the easy part. <strong>Maintaining it</strong> — storing seeds safely, tracking accounts, remembering passphrases and derivation details — is <strong>the real struggle</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="origin-prose">
          <p>
            One seed is hard enough. <strong>Multiple seeds</strong>, multiple accounts under each, <strong>passphrases</strong> on top of those — and suddenly you are not only securing coins. You are securing a map: which <strong>index</strong>, which <strong>derivation path</strong>, which passphrase belongs where.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where do you put that map? In a <strong>book</strong> — someone finds it, or water and time destroy it. On a <strong>phone</strong> — malware and backups. On a “clean” spare phone — you still do not truly know what runs on it. On an <strong>air-gapped laptop</strong> writing to USB — the machine can still leave traces you never intended. Invent your own <strong>cipher alphabet</strong> — easy to forget; write the rules down — easy for someone else to reverse.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="origin-almost">
          <h3>The almost-solution</h3>
          <p>
            There was a path that looked serious on paper: encrypted keypad USB, forever-offline phone, point-and-shoot camera with MicroSD, standalone CD duplicator, portable CD/DVD player, printer with USB — then <strong>split each 24-word seed into quarters</strong> across those media. First six words photographed to MicroSD. Next six onto the encrypted stick and burned to disc. Next six from an air-gapped editor onto ordinary USBs. Last six written, scanned, copied, printer wiped. <strong>Multiple copies. Multiple hiding places.</strong> A ritual to reassemble when restore day came.
          </p>
          <p>
            It felt complete — until you lived with it. <strong>Too many parts. Too many locations.</strong> Heat, moisture, and electrostatic discharge waiting to <strong>corrupt</strong> MicroSD, USB, and discs. SeedQR helps portability, but unprotected (or even “protected”) media still get <strong>lost, damaged, or stolen</strong> as physical objects.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="origin-need">
          <p class="origin-need-label">What was actually needed</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Something that does not silently rot</li>
            <li>Physical and portable</li>
            <li>Easy to recover when life goes wrong</li>
            <li>Highly secure</li>
            <li>Writable — a real backup you can hold</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="origin-prose">
          <p>
            Hiring that into existence costs real money and rare trust. The other path: <strong>build it yourself</strong>. The idea of how it must work was already clear — even before the code. With AI and many iterations, <strong>SeedMask took shape</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The core bargain is simple and strict: a writable <strong>backup code</strong> plus <strong>your own password</strong> always derives your seed the same way. <strong>No maybe. No lucky guesses.</strong> Strong password discipline and careful password storage are still on you — but the recovery path is <strong>deterministic</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            You can carry the backup code. Leave it in a drawer. Entrust it to someone careful — or even someone you do not fully trust — <em>if</em> the password is strong enough that <strong>the code alone is useless</strong>. That is not a license to be reckless with photos and printouts. It is a measure of how the design is meant to work: <strong>the backup code is portable; the password is the gate</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="origin-close">
          SeedMask was born because the DIY vault stack was <strong>too fragile for real life</strong> — and self-custody deserved something <strong>physical, recoverable</strong>, and honest about where the risk still sits.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <article class="name-story">
        <p class="eyebrow">The name</p>
        <h2>Why “SeedMask”</h2>
        <div class="name-story-grid">
          <div>
            <h3>It masks your seed</h3>
            <p>
              Your seed phrase is the master key to your funds. SeedMask is designed so that seed never leaves the device — in the final hardware form, the plan is that it stays masked inside SeedMask and never gets out onto a computer, phone, or the network.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Safer to store, easier to live with</h3>
            <p>
              Masking the seed is not only about keeping it offline. It is about storing it better, safer, and more conveniently — so you can use Bitcoin and Kaspa without writing secrets into everyday apps or leaving recovery words exposed.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="name-story-note">
          At first, SeedMask can sound dangerous or even contradictory — as if something were being hidden from you. That is far from the truth. The name is a promise of protection: SeedMask aims to become a wallet that guards your seed, generates it safely and with high entropy, and keeps it masked from the places that leak — so the product can live up to what it is called.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container about-grid">
      <article class="about-card">
        <span class="pillar-index">Vision</span>
        <h3>Keys stay on SeedMask</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Keys on device. Watch-only on desktop. Signing stays offline.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          SeedMask is the vault you hold: seed generation, storage, and signing stay on the hardware — masked from phones, browsers, and everyday machines that were never built to keep a seed safe.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="about-card">
        <span class="pillar-index">Focus</span>
        <h3>Bitcoin &amp; Kaspa only</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Satoshi’s vision. Two coins means clearer signing screens and a smaller attack surface than a multi-coin wallet.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Every extra chain adds formats, fees, and confirmation edge cases at the moment you should be careful. SeedMask stays narrow on purpose — Bitcoin and Kaspa done properly, not a crowded catalog of half-supported assets.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="about-card">
        <span class="pillar-index">Stance</span>
        <h3>100% open-source</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Firmware, Coordinator, and the full signing path — published so anyone can audit it.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Closed wallets ask you to trust a black box. SeedMask is built so independent eyes can inspect how keys are handled, how updates ship, and how the air-gapped path actually works — transparency as a custody requirement, not a slogan.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <article class="security-model" id="security-model">
        <p class="eyebrow">Security model</p>
        <h2>How SeedMask protects a seed</h2>
        <p class="security-lead">
          The seed never leaves the device to a phone or a coordinator. Signing and backup stay on the hardware. You hold the words, the backup code, and the password. What this board can and cannot do is below — including entropy and the missing secure element.
        </p>

        <div class="security-block" id="seed-entropy">
          <h3>Seed entropy</h3>
          <div class="origin-prose">
            <p>
              A seed is only as strong as the randomness that produced it. Weak entropy still looks like a normal 12/24-word phrase. The defect is invisible.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Standard</strong> uses the ESP32’s built-in RNG. Radios stay off, so the firmware briefly feeds analog ADC noise into that RNG, then fills the entropy bytes. That is chip RNG plus software. It is <strong>unmeasured</strong>. It is <strong>not a certified true-random generator</strong>. There is no second mixer and no health tests yet. Treat Standard as convenience, not a proven entropy source.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Dice roll</strong> is the honest Create path on this firmware: a physical d6 on the device (50 rolls for 12 words, 99 for 24). The rolls are hashed into BIP39 entropy. The randomness is yours, not the chip’s.
            </p>
            <p>
              Use Standard for burner seeds and testing. For a seed you intend to keep, use dice — or restore words generated another way you trust.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="security-block" id="no-secure-element">
          <h3>No secure element</h3>
          <div class="origin-prose">
            <p>
              This preview board is a general ESP32-S3. There is <strong>no secure element</strong>. The seed and vault live in ordinary RAM and ordinary flash on a chip that was not built to hide keys from someone who holds the board.
            </p>
            <p>
              Without an SE, keys are not sealed in dedicated silicon, and signing is not confined to tamper-resistant hardware. Software encryption (the password vault) is only as strong as the password. Flash Encryption and Secure Boot are <strong>not enabled</strong> on this generation — a dump of a live vault can still be useful, and firmware is not locked to a signed image.
            </p>
            <p>
              Wipe and Stateless are what <em>this firmware</em> does to data on the chip. They do not turn the MCU into a secure element. The <a href="#physical-security">retail hardware plan</a> puts keys in an SE. This board is not that product. Do not put life savings on it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="security-block">
          <h3>Backup code</h3>
          <div class="origin-prose">
            <p>
              A backup code is a password-wrapped copy of the seed you can write down or scan. It is not the 12/24 words, and it is not a full vault dump.
            </p>
            <p>
              New codes use SeedMask’s current format (SPB1 v2). Older codes from before this format will not restore — make a new code after you update firmware.
            </p>
            <p>
              The wrap is a 100,000-iteration password KDF, XOR of the packed seed, and a short HMAC so a mistyped or altered code fails instead of silently unlocking junk. On the chip, the live vault still uses AES-GCM. The paper/QR code is kept short on purpose.
            </p>
            <p>
              Creating a code requires a password of at least 12 characters, with uppercase, a digit, and a symbol. Obvious and padded-cliché passwords are rejected. That floor is for <strong>creating</strong> a backup code. Lock PIN and vault unlock are separate.
            </p>
            <p>
              After you create a seed backup, the QR on that screen is the backup code itself: the same SPB1 string as the boxed characters you write down. One backup, one QR. Scan Restore, enter that backup password, and you get the seed — not the password manager, notes, or 2FA.
            </p>
            <p>
              Seed Settings also has a separate <strong>Vault QR</strong>. That is the encrypted vault: denser, often several frames, a different payload. Restoring it brings back the seed <em>and</em> what’s in the vault (passwords, notes, 2FA). It is not the backup-code QR, which is seed-only. View Seed’s QR is something else again: the words themselves, unprotected.
            </p>
            <p>
              A strong, unique password is what actually gates a stolen slip. Do not photograph the code, send it in chat, or store it in cloud photos. USB <strong>Send</strong> only types into whatever window is focused on a plugged-in computer; that is a transfer tool, not permission to host the code online.
            </p>
            <p>
              If you lose the password, the backup code cannot be restored. Keep the original words as well.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="security-block">
          <h3>Wipe</h3>
          <div class="origin-prose">
            <p>
              Wipe Device securely clears <strong>this unit’s data flash</strong> (NVS and SPIFFS, and FAT / coredump if present): erase, overwrite with random, erase again. After it finishes, a dump of those partitions has no seed and no vault to recover — on the chip, there is nothing to find.
            </p>
            <p>
              Firmware stays so the device can boot. The microSD is not touched — that card is yours to destroy or keep.
            </p>
            <p>
              Wipe does not reach paper, photos, or another machine. That is copies, not leftover flash.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="security-block">
          <h3>Stateless</h3>
          <div class="origin-prose">
            <p>
              Stateless means this session’s wallet is <strong>not written</strong> to the chip. Create and restore stay in RAM only. That is “don’t save,” not “destroy a wallet that was already stored.”
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>After you reboot (or power off) in Stateless, this unit no longer holds the seed.</strong> RAM is gone with power. Nothing was saved to flash, so there is no vault waiting on the next boot — you start clean.
            </p>
            <p>
              While the device is still on and unlocked, the seed <em>is</em> in RAM (same as Stateful). The empty-device moment is <strong>reboot / power off</strong>, not the seconds you are using it.
            </p>
            <p>
              If a vault is already saved on the device, Stateless will not pretend it is gone. Removing a stored wallet is <strong>Wipe</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="security-in-short">
          <p class="origin-need-label">In short</p>
          <div class="security-table-wrap">
            <table class="security-use-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>You want…</th>
                  <th>Use…</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>A seed whose randomness you can stand behind</td>
                  <td>Dice roll — not Standard</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>This chip to resist dump / chip-off</td>
                  <td>It can’t — no SE, no Flash Encryption</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>This session not saved to the chip — gone after reboot</td>
                  <td>Stateless</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>A stored wallet erased from this chip (flash dump finds nothing)</td>
                  <td>Wipe</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>A portable wrap of the seed</td>
                  <td>Backup code + a strong unique password + the words</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="security-close">
          This generation is a preview board: a real key-handling design on hardware that cannot protect like retail (no secure element, no Secure Boot, no Flash Encryption). Entropy, the missing SE, backup-code wrap, wipe, and Stateless above are how SeedMask behaves <strong>now</strong>.
          The ESP32-S3 on this board has Wi-Fi and Bluetooth. Firmware never turns those radios on — signing is QR and MicroSD — but that is software policy, not a proof they cannot radiate or be switched on.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band roadmap-section" id="roadmap">
    <div class="container">
